"summary": "The average degree of a finite simple graph is the sum of its vertex degrees divided by the number of vertices (equivalently twice the edge count over the vertex count). Mader conjectured in 1979 that every graph of average degree at least 3k-1 contains a (k+1)-connected subgraph; the subgraph may be taken induced, since adding edges preserves vertex connectivity. The conjecture has been open for almost half a century: Mader proved in 1972 that average degree 4k-2 suffices, the known sufficient linear factor was improved over the years but never reached 3, and Krone proved the minimum-degree analogue (minimum degree 3k-1 forces a (k+1)-connected subgraph on more than 2k vertices). If true, the bound 3k-1 is sharp for every value of the core connectivity.",
