"summary": "A perfect matching of a multigraph is an edge set meeting every vertex in exactly one edge. The Berge-Fulkerson conjecture (Fulkerson 1971) asserts that every bridgeless cubic multigraph has six perfect matchings that together cover every edge exactly twice - equivalently, doubling every edge yields a 6-edge-colourable multigraph. For 3-edge-colourable cubic graphs the six matchings are the three colour classes taken twice, so any counterexample is a snark; the Petersen graph and all verified snark families satisfy the conjecture. By Mazzuoccolo's theorem it is equivalent to Berge's conjecture that five perfect matchings suffice to cover every edge at least once, and it implies the Fan-Raspaud conjecture that three perfect matchings can avoid a common edge.",
