"summary": "First-order ZFC is deep-embedded in Lean: the language has a single binary membership relation, the eight core axioms and the separation and replacement schemas are spelled out as de Bruijn sentences, and CH is the sentence that every subset of P(omega) injects into omega or receives an injection from P(omega). The problem is three-optioned - prove CH from ZFC, refute it, or prove independence - and exactly one option is provable: the futility certificate. It is unconditional, since Lean's universes provide models of the embedded ZFC; a kernel-checked proof of either other option would witness an inconsistency of Lean itself. Goedel (1938) and Cohen (1963) supply the two model constructions, and the Flypitch project (Han-van Doorn, CPP 2020) formalized exactly this independence in Lean 3, so the problem is solved-in-principle at research level. Consequence is semantic (Mathlib has no syntactic calculus), which agrees with provability by Goedel completeness.",
