"summary": "A bipartite graph is presented by the neighbourhood function of its left side. A right vertex is a unique neighbour of a set S of left vertices if it is adjacent to exactly one member of S, and a (c,d)-biregular unique-neighbour expander family is a uniform threshold delta > 0 together with (c,d)-biregular graphs of arbitrarily large order in which every nonempty set of at most delta|L| left vertices has a unique neighbour. Asherov and Dinur (2023) prove that there is a threshold function q-hat such that for every integer c0 > 5, real alpha > 1 and prime power q > q-hat(c0, alpha) with alpha c0 (q+1) an integer, there is a (c0(q+1), alpha c0 (q+1))-biregular unique-neighbour expander family - arbitrarily unbalanced sides - constructed in polynomial time by composing (q+1, q^3+1)-biregular Ramanujan graphs with a fixed-size gadget through a routed product. The formal statement records the mathematical existence with the paper's degree parameters; explicitness is not captured. Known true at research level: the paper's route needs a new average-degree bound for bipartite Ramanujan graphs, and the non-constructive route needs random biregular graph expansion. (Formalisation upgraded 2026-09-07: the challenge file now defines bipartite multigraphs, the routed product and Lemma 12 itself; the previous thin statement is kept in the attic.)",
