"summary": "A superpermutation on n symbols is a word over an n-letter alphabet containing every permutation of the alphabet as a contiguous factor. In 2011 an anonymous poster on 4chan proved that every superpermutation on n symbols has length at least n! + (n-1)! + (n-2)! + n - 3, improving the Ashlock-Tillotson bound n! + (n-1)! + n - 2; the proof was written up by Houston, Pantone and Vatter in 2018. The bound is tight for n <= 4 (minimal lengths 1, 3, 9, 33) and off by one for n = 5 (minimum 153). The proof counts wasted letters: a letter is wasted unless it completes the first occurrence of a permutation; the first n-1 letters are wasted, each of the (n-1)! cyclic classes of permutations forces a wasted letter upon completion, and each of the (n-2)! trajectories - chains of n-1 cyclic classes linked by single wasted letters - forces a further, distinct wasted letter to change trajectory. The formal statement needs no hypothesis on n, natural-number arithmetic making the small cases trivial. Known true; the combinatorial proof has not been formalized.",
