"summary": "A Latin square of order n is an n-by-n array of n symbols with no repeated symbol in any row or column; its sign is the product of the signs of its n row permutations and n column permutations, and it is even or odd accordingly. The Alon-Tarsi conjecture (1992) asserts that for every even n the numbers of even and odd Latin squares of order n differ. For odd n >= 3 the two numbers are equal, since swapping two rows is a sign-reversing involution. The conjecture is known for n = p+1 (Drisko 1997) and n = p-1 (Glynn 2010) with p an odd prime, hence for every even n <= 24; the smallest open case is n = 26. By Huang and Rota it implies Rota's basis conjecture in even dimension over characteristic zero, and via the Alon-Tarsi polynomial method it implies the Dinitz conjecture for even n. The signed count is usually computed over row-reduced squares (first row in natural order), which for even n differs from the full signed count by the factor n!.",
